{"id":3901,"date":"2021-10-25T13:54:36","date_gmt":"2021-10-25T20:54:36","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=3901"},"modified":"2025-10-23T13:28:41","modified_gmt":"2025-10-23T20:28:41","slug":"2-3-variables-senior-cde-blockly","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-3-variables-senior-cde-blockly\/","title":{"rendered":"2.3: Variables"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22135ac180f\" name=\"6a22135ac180f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>We've Moved!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1761251317979{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-11766\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"81\" height=\"82\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>We\u2019ve moved to our new lesson portal: <a href=\"http:\/\/learn.robolink.com\">Robolink Learn<\/a>! Lessons here will remain available until the end of June 2026. This version of Basecamp will no longer be updated and uses outdated references to Blockly for Robolink. You can access the previous version of <a href=\"https:\/\/codrone.robolink.com\/edu\/blockly-archive\/\">Blockly here<\/a>.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22135ac19b0\" name=\"6a22135ac19b0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are Variables?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<strong>Variables<\/strong> are a way to store information in a program, and they\u2019re used most often to store values. If you\u2019re playing a video game, there\u2019s a variable to store your score, another one for your lives, and maybe another one for experience points. With your CoDrone EDU, you might use them to store the value of a sensor, the speed of your drone, or maybe how far it will go.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2977&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Variables are especially beneficial if this value appears multiple times in your program. Take a look at the following square code.<em> What would you have to do to make the square bigger? How can a variable solve this problem?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10249&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22135ac1a51\" name=\"6a22135ac1a51\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Create a Variable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]To create a variable, go to the &#8220;Variables&#8221; menu in Blockly and click on the\u00a0<strong>Create variable&#8230;<\/strong> button.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12900&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Next, you have to name your variable. In programming, it is important that your variable name is short and descriptive. For now, we will use a common variable you have probably used in your math class before. Name your variable &#8220;x&#8221;.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]For every new variable you create, you will see a set of blocks created in the &#8220;Variable&#8221; menu for you to use.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22135ac1adb\" name=\"6a22135ac1adb\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Initializing a Variable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]To initialize a variable means to assign the variable to a value. In this course, you will normally set variables to a number, but there are other\u00a0variable types\u00a0as well that you will learn about in a future lesson. Drag the <strong>set<\/strong>\u00a0block with your variable to the workspace.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;5984&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Set the variable &#8220;x&#8221; to a number by accessing the &#8220;Math&#8221; menu and dragging in the number block.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12681&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Then, change the 0 value to any number<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;5604&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22135ac1b6f\" name=\"6a22135ac1b6f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Access the Variable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]When you need to use the value that is stored in the variable, you can use the block with the variable name. In the example below, we are setting a new variable &#8220;y&#8221; to 5.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12902&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22135ac1bf8\" name=\"6a22135ac1bf8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Print the Variable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]A good tool to know for debugging variable values is the\u00a0<strong>print<\/strong>\u00a0block. The <strong>print<\/strong> block can be found inside the &#8220;Input\/Output&#8221; menu.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12682&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Connect a variable or any block that returns a value to see the value in the console window. Add a <strong>print<\/strong>\u00a0block to your program and connect it to the block that retrieves the &#8220;y&#8221; value.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12903&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22135ac1ca2\" name=\"6a22135ac1ca2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using Variables in Flight<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that you know the basics of using variables, let&#8217;s use variables to improve the square program. Open or create a program that will fly CoDrone EDU in a square shape. Let&#8217;s use the example from Step 2.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10249&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Run your code and observe its behavior. Then, create a new variable &#8220;seconds&#8221; and set it to 1 at the top of your square program.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12904&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Find every part in your code that uses the number 1 to program a flight duration. Now that you have a variable, you can replace each of these duration\u00a0parameters\u00a0with the <strong>variable<\/strong> block for seconds. Drag and drop the <strong>variable<\/strong> block directly into the seconds parameter for each block.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12905&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Run the code again and notice that it runs the exact same way as before!<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22135ac1d39\" name=\"6a22135ac1d39\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Change the Variable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<em>What would you do to make the square bigger?<\/em> In the original program without variables, you would need to change every\u00a0<strong>move()<\/strong> block. Now, you only need to change the variable value, and it will be applied for the entire program. Make the square bigger by changing the value to 2. Make sure to clear a space that&#8217;s large enough for your new flight path.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12906&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22135ac1dcb\" name=\"6a22135ac1dcb\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Spiraling Square<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]To change a variable value in the middle of a program without user input, you can use the <strong>change by<\/strong>\u00a0block. This block will increase or decrease the value of the variable by the number you input. For example, this program increases the length of each side by 0.5 seconds for each side of the square. Draw the flight path on paper. <em>What does it look like?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12907&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22135ac1e4e\" name=\"6a22135ac1e4e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: More variables!<\/b><\/p>\n<p>From your square program, add other variables for duration, power, or degrees. <em>What kinds of flight patterns can you create?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12908&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22135ac1ec8\" name=\"6a22135ac1ec8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 11<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Let\u2019s review what you learned about variables so far! You can use variables to hold values for the CoDrone EDU to use in the program. In the activities above, you got to see how to save values in a variable and use them with other blocks.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7547&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 12 We've Moved! [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1761251317979{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] We\u2019ve moved to our new lesson portal: Robolink Learn! Lessons here&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-3-variables-senior-cde-blockly\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4739,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3877,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1814,1817,1763,1777,1737,1793,626,1533,1438,853,144,167,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2108,4